n{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":4,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"input_json_delta\",\"partial_json\":\"{\\\"file_path\\\": \\\"/Users/leemoore/.claude/\"}},\"session_id\":\"8d6d1f63-412d-4ec0-a1d3-5b6b004b453c\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"48fae904-efbf-4866-b93f-a92856f209fa\"}\n{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":4,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"input_json_delta\",\"partial_json\":\"projects/-Users-leemoore-code-pi-long-horizon-liminal-context/memory/lbuild-impl-needs-ruling-on-impl\"}},\"session_id\":\"8d6d1f63-412d-4ec0-a1d3-5b6b004b453c\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"b972b967-732e-4c50-9d25-41fc97c4dfd1\"}\n{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01LrCS88Q25MnWXE2huCpuR3\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t---\\n2\\tname: pi-lhc-epic-01-connector-core-build\\n3\\tdescription: Autonomous orchestration (impl-lead) of the pi-lhc Epic 1 Connector Core build via lbuild-impl ls-impl; durable run state lives in team-impl-log.md.\\n4\\tmetadata: \\n5\\t  node_type: memory\\n6\\t  type: project\\n7\\t  originSessionId: c60a53bf-54ba-4447-8e83-5ae7d2b4edb8\\n8\\t---\\n9\\t\\n10\\tLee tasked an autonomous, long-horizon orchestration (I am impl-lead) of the **pi-lhc Epic 1 \\\"Connector Core\\\"** build using the `lbuild-impl` CLI + `ls-impl` skill. Spec pack root: `docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/` (epic.md, tech-design.md, test-plan.md, stories 00-foundation … 06-startup-validation). pi-lhc is a PI extension that hosts the LHC SDK (`packages/pi-lhc`, workspace-depends on `packages/lhc`). Epic 1 is **observe-only**: capture PI hook traffic into LHC threads, derive LHC turns from PI traffic (PI fires turn-per-step; one LHC turn = whole exchange, closed at agent_end), route derivation inference through PI's auth — change nothing the model sees.\\n11\\t\\n12\\t**Why:** Multi-story provider-backed build spanning hours; state must survive context compaction and session restarts, so it is kept on disk, not in conversation.\\n13\\t\\n14\\t**How to apply:** On resume, read `docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/team-impl-log.md` FIRST (it holds State, Current Story, Current Phase, story receipts, baselines, continuation handles) plus `impl-run.config.json` and `artifacts/`, and continue from the recorded State — do NOT reconstruct from memory of prior turns. Provider config: story-lead+verifier+quick-fixer = codex/gpt-5.5, implementor = claude-opus-4-8/max (codex auth confirmed working). Story gate `pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify`; epic gate `pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify-all`. Stories touching `packages/lhc` (e.g. Story 1's A-8 registry additions) also need `pnpm --filter lhc verify` at acceptance since configured gates are pi-lhc-scoped. Commit per accepted story on `main` with scoped pathspecs — a large pre-existing lhc-docs reorg (~1267 entries) sits staged in the index; keep it OUT of story commits. Long provider runs: background them and watch with a ~5-min Monitor on the status.json until terminal. See [[lbuild-impl-needs-ruling-on-implementor-deviations]].\\n15\\t\\n16\\t**Recurring failure modes + recovery (observed across Stories 1-2):** (1) SILENCE STALL — a story-continue resuming a large claude-code session goes silent and hits the silence timeout; fixed by raising `story_implementor_silence_timeout_ms` to 1200000 (20m) in impl-run.config.json (done). (2) LARGE-OUTPUT PARSE FAILURE — claude-code provider operations (implement OR continue) that emit more than ~1MB of streaming stdout fail at finalize with `PROVIDER_OUTPUT_INVALID` (\\\"stdout was not exact JSON\\\"; the runtime stops the process as 'stale' and can't parse the result envelope). This blocks from-scratch implements/continues on LARGE stories. CRITICAL: claude-code APPLIES EDITS LIVE as it streams, so even when the result envelope fails to parse, the code changes ARE on disk. RECOVERY for the large-output case: do NOT keep retrying ever-larger implement/continue ops. Instead (a) gate the on-disk state (`pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify`) to confirm it's coherent, then (b) run a FRESH primitive `story-verify` (verifier output is bounded, so it finalizes fine) to get an authoritative verdict on the on-disk code, then (c) accept if it passes, or use `quick-fix` (small bounded output) for focused remaining findings + re-verify. Drive acceptance with impl-lead authority (receipt + commit) outside the stuck composed loop when needed. (3) Transient codex story-lead PLANNER output-invalid on a turn — a plain `story-orchestrate resume` retries the planner turn. (4) Broken/unstable session resume — reopen review-request directing a fresh run-implement. (5) CODEX RESUME DRIFT — a codex story-verify FOLLOW-UP (resume) fails PROVIDER_OUTPUT_INVALID \\\"Codex resume schema drift\\\" (resume can't take --output-schema); for re-verification always use a FRESH story-verify (initial mode) with post-fix context, never a follow-up. (6) For fixes when claude-code can't finalize (>1MB), the CODEX quick_fixer + a fresh codex story-verify is the reliable loop (codex doesn't share claude's size bug). When a verifier keeps surfacing ever-narrower edges of the SAME finding, stop piecemeal-patching: reason about whether the failing input is reachable in PRODUCTION (check the real PI hook contract in notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md) — real PI message_end ALWAYS carries a stable entry id (research line ~142 \\\"every entry carries id+parentId\\\"), so id-less/position-less collision repros are synthetic/unreachable and can be accepted-risk with a hardening note rather than chased forever.\\n17\\t\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"8d6d1f63-412d-4ec0-a1d3-5b6b004b453c\",\"uuid\":\"f38c554f-8afa-420e-80ef-23b8c32a1c6d\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-14T10:44:56.150Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/leemoore/.claude/projects/-Users-leemoore-code-pi-long-horizon-liminal-context/memory/pi-lhc-epic-01-connector-core-build.md\",\"content\":\"---\\nname: pi-lhc-epic-01-connector-core-build\\ndescription: Autonomous orchestration (impl-lead) of the pi-lhc Epic 1 Connector Core build via lbuild-impl ls-impl; durable run state lives in team-impl-log.md.\\nmetadata: \\n  node_type: memory\\n  type: project\\n  originSessionId: c60a53bf-54ba-4447-8e83-5ae7d2b4edb8\\n---\\n\\nLee tasked an autonomous, long-horizon orchestration (I am impl-lead) of the **pi-lhc Epic 1 \\\"Connector Core\\\"** build using the `lbuild-impl` CLI + `ls-impl` skill. Spec pack root: `docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/` (epic.md, tech-design.md, test-plan.md, stories 00-foundation … 06-startup-validation). pi-lhc is a PI extension that hosts the LHC SDK (`packages/pi-lhc`, workspace-depends on `packages/lhc`). Epic 1 is **observe-only**: capture PI hook traffic into LHC threads, derive LHC turns from PI traffic (PI fires turn-per-step; one LHC turn = whole exchange, closed at agent_end), route derivation inference through PI's auth — change nothing the model sees.\\n\\n**Why:** Multi-story provider-backed build spanning hours; state must survive context compaction and session restarts, so it is kept on disk, not in conversation.\\n\\n**How to apply:** On resume, read `docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/team-impl-log.md` FIRST (it holds State, Current Story, Current Phase, story receipts, baselines, continuation handles) plus `impl-run.config.json` and `artifacts/`, and continue from the recorded State — do NOT reconstruct from memory of prior turns. Provider config: story-lead+verifier+quick-fixer = codex/gpt-5.5, implementor = claude-opus-4-8/max (codex auth confirmed working). Story gate `pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify`; epic gate `pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify-all`. Stories touching `packages/lhc` (e.g. Story 1's A-8 registry additions) also need `pnpm --filter lhc verify` at acceptance since configured gates are pi-lhc-scoped. Commit per accepted story on `main` with scoped pathspecs — a large pre-existing lhc-docs reorg (~1267 entries) sits staged in the index; keep it OUT of story commits. Long provider runs: background them and watch with a ~5-min Monitor on the status.json until terminal. See [[lbuild-impl-needs-ruling-on-implementor-deviations]].\\n\\n**Recurring failure modes + recovery (observed across Stories 1-2):** (1) SILENCE STALL — a story-continue resuming a large claude-code session goes silent and hits the silence timeout; fixed by raising `story_implementor_silence_timeout_ms` to 1200000 (20m) in impl-run.config.json (done). (2) LARGE-OUTPUT PARSE FAILURE — claude-code provider operations (implement OR continue) that emit more than ~1MB of streaming stdout fail at finalize with `PROVIDER_OUTPUT_INVALID` (\\\"stdout was not exact JSON\\\"; the runtime stops the process as 'stale' and can't parse the result envelope). This blocks from-scratch implements/continues on LARGE stories. CRITICAL: claude-code APPLIES EDITS LIVE as it streams, so even when the result envelope fails to parse, the code changes ARE on disk. RECOVERY for the large-output case: do NOT keep retrying ever-larger implement/continue ops. Instead (a) gate the on-disk state (`pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify`) to confirm it's coherent, then (b) run a FRESH primitive `story-verify` (verifier output is bounded, so it finalizes fine) to get an authoritative verdict on the on-disk code, then (c) accept if it passes, or use `quick-fix` (small bounded output) for focused remaining findings + re-verify. Drive acceptance with impl-lead authority (receipt + commit) outside the stuck composed loop when needed. (3) Transient codex story-lead PLANNER output-invalid on a turn — a plain `story-orchestrate resume` retries the planner turn. (4) Broken/unstable session resume — reopen review-request directing a fresh run-implement. (5) CODEX RESUME DRIFT — a codex story-verify FOLLOW-UP (resume) fails PROVIDER_OUTPUT_INVALID \\\"Codex resume schema drift\\\" (resume can't take --output-schema); for re-verification always use a FRESH story-verify (initial mode) with post-fix context, never a follow-up. (6) For fixes when claude-code can't finalize (>1MB), the CODEX quick_fixer + a fresh codex story-verify is the reliable loop (codex doesn't share claude's size bug). When a verifier keeps surfacing ever-narrower edges of the SAME finding, stop piecemeal-patching: reason about whether the failing input is reachable in PRODUCTION (check the real PI hook contract in notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md) — real PI message_end ALWAYS carries a stable entry id (research line ~142 \\\"every entry carries id+parentId\\\"), so id-less/position-less collision repros are synthetic/unreachable and can be accepted-risk with a hardening note rather than chased forever.\\n\",\"numLines\":17,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":17}}}\n{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":4,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"input_json_delta\",\"partial_json\":\"ementor-deviations.md\"}},\"session_id\":\"8d6d1f63-412d-4ec0-a1d3-5b6b004b453c\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"a660a6e1-ecc5-49d8-af02-6a24bd814856\"}\n{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":4,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"input_json_delta\",\"partial_json\":\"\\\"}\"}},\"session_id\":\"8d6d1f63-412d-4ec0-a1d3-5b6b004b453c\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"d55e3678-0c2b-4ea3-9644-76830fb36f81\"}\n{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-8\",\"id\":\"msg_011cVEu6uXVBWzNvkVQjdTXa\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_01FAf8wWJpZqryEHjb8Ekr6C\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/leemoore/.claude/projects/-Users-leemoore-code-pi-long-horizon-liminal-context/memory/lbuild-impl-needs-ruling-on-implementor-deviations.md\"},\"caller\":{\"type\":\"direct\"}}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":37365,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":20937,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":37365},\"output_tokens\":3,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"8d6d1f63-412d-4ec0-a1d3-5b6b004b453c\",\"uuid\":\"91f7f015-0275-4f04-b835-ee391ab39d14\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cc31t7CChD9jCJKEfSk7t\"}\n{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_stop\",\"index\":4},\"session_id\":\"8d6d1f63-412d-4ec0-a1d3-5b6b004b453c\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"d2b4e39f-a69e-49dc-8fae-11f599d5994c\"}\n{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"message_delta\",\"delta\":{\"stop_reason\":\"tool_use\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null},\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":37365,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":20937,\"output_tokens\":776,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"thinking_tokens\":367},\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":2,\"output_tokens\":776,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":20937,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":37365,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":37365},\"type\":\"message\"}]},\"context_management\":{\"applied_edits\":[]}},\"session_id\":\"8d6d1f63-412d-4ec0-a1d3-5b6b004b453c\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"a951059f-e195-4c2b-9469-b45ecd1af0f4\"}\n{\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01FAf8wWJpZqryEHjb8Ekr6C\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"1\\t---\\n2\\tname: lbuild-impl-needs-ruling-on-implementor-deviations\\n3\\tdescription: \\\"lbuild-impl story-orchestrate ALWAYS terminates needs-ruling when the implementor surfaces a spec deviation; resolve by impl-lead direct acceptance, never loop resume.\\\"\\n4\\tmetadata: \\n5\\t  node_type: memory\\n6\\t  type: project\\n7\\t  originSessionId: c60a53bf-54ba-4447-8e83-5ae7d2b4edb8\\n8\\t---\\n9\\t\\n10\\tIn the lbuild-impl / lspec-core `ls-impl` runtime, when a story implementor surfaces ANY spec deviation, `buildStoryLeadFinalPackage` (lspec-core `dist/bin/lbuild-impl.js`, ~L13567) re-injects each implementor-surfaced deviation into the final package with `approvalStatus: \\\"needs-ruling\\\"` on EVERY package build, unconditionally. A caller ruling delivered via `story-orchestrate resume --ruling-file` IS durably recorded (callerInputHistory.rulings + acceptedRulingArtifact) but does NOT mutate those per-item approvalStatus values; `reverifyRulingRequest` re-derives the ruling request purely from items still marked `needs-ruling`, and `resolvedOutcome` then forces an `accept-story` planner action down to `needs-ruling`. Net effect: `story-orchestrate run`/`resume` ALWAYS terminates `needs-ruling` for a story whose implementor surfaced a deviation. The story-lead loop structurally cannot self-clear it.\\n11\\t\\n12\\t**Why:** By design — acceptance is impl-lead authority; the CLI/story-lead never accepts a story or self-approves a deviation. The `needs-ruling` terminal is the loop correctly handing the deviation decision to the orchestrator (me).\\n13\\t\\n14\\t**How to apply:** Do NOT loop `story-orchestrate resume` hoping for an `accepted` terminal — it will return `needs-ruling` forever and waste provider turns/tokens. Instead: (1) evaluate the deviation from evidence (verify any factual claims it makes); (2) deliver the ruling once via `resume --ruling-file` as the durable decision record — JSON schema is `.strict()` `{ rulingRequestId, decision, rationale, source }` (decision ∈ the request's allowedResponses, e.g. \\\"approve\\\"/\\\"reject\\\"); (3) then ACCEPT THE STORY DIRECTLY as impl-lead: run the recorded story gate yourself, write the complete receipt in `team-impl-log.md` (disposition the deviation as accepted-risk or fixed), and land the story commit. That sequence IS acceptance per the ls-impl operating model — a `needs-ruling` story-orchestrate terminal is not a blocker to impl-lead acceptance. Step (2)'s ruling can even be skipped if you record the decision directly in the receipt, but delivering it leaves a clean durable artifact. See [[pi-lhc-epic-01-connector-core-build]].\\n15\\t\"}]},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"8d6d1f63-412d-4ec0-a1d3-5b6b004b453c\",\"uuid\":\"599f27a7-7c34-41ba-8724-fdde32abcbcd\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-06-14T10:44:56.809Z\",\"tool_use_result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":{\"filePath\":\"/Users/leemoore/.claude/projects/-Users-leemoore-code-pi-long-horizon-liminal-context/memory/lbuild-impl-needs-ruling-on-implementor-deviations.md\",\"content\":\"---\\nname: lbuild-impl-needs-ruling-on-implementor-deviations\\ndescription: \\\"lbuild-impl story-orchestrate ALWAYS terminates needs-ruling when the implementor surfaces a spec deviation; resolve by impl-lead direct acceptance, never loop resume.\\\"\\nmetadata: \\n  node_type: memory\\n  type: project\\n  originSessionId: c60a53bf-54ba-4447-8e83-5ae7d2b4edb8\\n---\\n\\nIn the lbuild-impl / lspec-core `ls-impl` runtime, when a story implementor surfaces ANY spec deviation, `buildStoryLeadFinalPackage` (lspec-core `dist/bin/lbuild-impl.js`, ~L13567) re-injects each implementor-surfaced deviation into the final package with `approvalStatus: \\\"needs-ruling\\\"` on EVERY package build, unconditionally. A caller ruling delivered via `story-orchestrate resume --ruling-file` IS durably recorded (callerInputHistory.rulings + acceptedRulingArtifact) but does NOT mutate those per-item approvalStatus values; `reverifyRulingRequest` re-derives the ruling request purely from items still marked `needs-ruling`, and `resolvedOutcome` then forces an `accept-story` planner action down to `needs-ruling`. Net effect: `story-orchestrate run`/`resume` ALWAYS terminates `needs-ruling` for a story whose implementor surfaced a deviation. The story-lead loop structurally cannot self-clear it.\\n\\n**Why:** By design — acceptance is impl-lead authority; the CLI/story-lead never accepts a story or self-approves a deviation. The `needs-ruling` terminal is the loop correctly handing the deviation decision to the orchestrator (me).\\n\\n**How to apply:** Do NOT loop `story-orchestrate resume` hoping for an `accepted` terminal — it will return `needs-ruling` forever and waste provider turns/tokens. Instead: (1) evaluate the deviation from evidence (verify any factual claims it makes); (2) deliver the ruling once via `resume --ruling-file` as the durable decision record — JSON schema is `.strict()` `{ rulingRequestId, decision, rationale, source }` (decision ∈ the request's allowedResponses, e.g. \\\"approve\\\"/\\\"reject\\\"); (3) then ACCEPT THE STORY DIRECTLY as impl-lead: run the recorded story gate yourself, write the complete receipt in `team-impl-log.md` (disposition the deviation as accepted-risk or fixed), and land the story commit. That sequence IS acceptance per the ls-impl operating model — a `needs-ruling` story-orchestrate terminal is not a blocker to impl-lead acceptance. Step (2)'s ruling can even be skipped if you record the decision directly in the receipt, but delivering it leaves a clean durable artifact. See [[pi-lhc-epic-01-connector-core-build]].\\n\",\"numLines\":15,\"startLine\":1,\"totalLines\":15}}}\n{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"message_stop\"},\"session_id\":\"8d6d1f63-412d-4ec0-a1d3-5b6b004b453c\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"672efd8f-5cd2-47c7-a2b5-4d8032f03185\"}\n{\"type\":\"system\",\"subtype\":\"status\",\"status\":\"requesting\",\"uuid\":\"4666e81f-9584-4de6-8773-88493bab6d00\",\"session_id\":\"8d6d1f63-412d-4ec0-a1d3-5b6b004b453c\"}\n{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"message_start\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-8\",\"id\":\"msg_01LqxPdYFVP11a8NpnSxPyt6\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\"